By FREE PRESS STAFF ,
5 hours ago
MONROE COUNTY — The Monroe County Tourism Development Council (TDC) is projected to spend more than $11 million on capital projects in fiscal year 2025-2026, providing grants to government entities and nonprofit organizations for infrastructure improvements, including repairs, renovations and upgrades, that enhance the Florida Keys as a destination for domestic and international tourism.
These improvements are funded through the tourist development tax, also known as the bed tax, paid by visitors for lodging.
Major projects include:
• City of Key West Mallory Square Wharf Improvements – more than $2.6 million for repairs to the Mallory Square seawall;
• Coral Restoration Foundation – more than $475,000 million for coral restoration;
• Friends of the Pool – $130,000 for continued preservation of Alligator Reef Light;
• Islamorada, Village of the Islands – more than $435,000 for the Founders Park pool resurfacing project;
• Monroe County Commission – more than $900,000 for Higgs Beach Pavilion Repairs, the Big Pine Community Park skate park and Jacobs Aquatic Center pool renovations;
• Monroe School Board Leasing Corp. – $500,000 for School Board Eleven Time Conch Cafe and Baseball Museum;
• Reef Environmental Education Foundation Center – more than $57,000 for outdoor attractions, a new roof and restoration to campus; and
• Reef Renewal USA – more than $360,000 for coral restoration.
“These projects directly support our mission to strengthen tourism infrastructure, while also delivering meaningful benefits to our local community,” said Kara Franker, Monroe County TDC president and CEO.
For the fiscal year beginning Oct. 1, the TDC accepted applications for the capital projects fund from February through April. The process for securing TDC funding begins with submitting a request for applications (RFA) to the TDC, which then forwards it to the District Advisory Committees (DACs) for initial review. In June, the DACs held funding meetings to evaluate proposals and voted on recommended funding amounts. These recommendations were then reviewed and approved by the TDC board, followed by final approval from the Monroe County Commission. Final approval is still ongoing with contracts being approved during the first quarter of the new fiscal year.
To be eligible, projects must primarily promote tourism and fall into one of the following categories: convention centers, sports stadiums, sports arenas, coliseums, auditoriums, aquariums, museums, zoological parks, nature centers, fishing piers, beach or beach park facilities, channels, estuaries, lagoons or other public facilities.
Additional organizations/entities awarded funding for projects in 2025-2026 include cities of Key Colony Beach and Marathon, Dolphin Research Center, Dolphins Plus Oceanside Marine Mammal Responder, Florida Keys Land & Sea Trust, Florida Keys Wild Bird Rehabilitation Center, Hidden Harbor Marine Environmental Project, I.CARE Corporation, Institute Patriotico y Docente San Carlos, Island Dolphin Care, Key West Botanical Garden Society, Marine Resources Development Foundation, Miami Dade Historical Maritime Museum, Old Island Restoration Foundation, Pigeon Key Foundation, Florida Keys History of Diving Museum and the Mel Fisher Maritime Heritage Society.
